I don't think so.  It could basically be replaced with two if's:

    if ( depth( &u.uz ) <=3D 0 ) {
        return false ;
    }
    if ( rn2( 1 + (depth( &u.z) / 4) ) =3D=3D 0 ) {
        return false ;
    }

I'm not sure, but I think that a depth <=3D 0 means the planes, so
the first condition prevents bones from the planes.  The second
generates a random integer in the half-open interval [0...x),
and says no bones if that is 0.  You'll note that for depth
between 0 and 3, integer arithmetic makes the expression
rn2(1+0), which will always return 0, and which is what actually
forbids bones from the first three levels.  Levels 4-7 block
bones 1/2 the time (so you get bones 1/2 the time), 8-11 block
1/3 of the time (so you get bones 2/3 of the times), and so on.
Down about level 24, you're leaving bones 6 times out of 7 (86%), and
at level 48, 12 times out of 13 (92%).

Except that in my case, I very rarely die after about level 10,
and practically never between the castle and the planes.  Which
means that I don't actually have any bones from those levels.

